ot on a self-comparison. The fenced record's `:yin.k/address` is the pack (the recovery object is `:yin.k/record`, `driver.cljc:916-919`), so the recovery object never enters `:packs`. The reversed-row variant is offered, never fenced, so it is excluded by construction (probe: one pack, two variants). On the process matrix the receiver life reads h1's durable fence and re-emits the source host's bytes: the cross-host claim is real there and only there, as codex says. Teeth: T1 (re-emit digest from reversed rows) fails 4 assertions in row 7 failure-lower; P6 (`pack` stops sorting) fails `:sorted?` 4 times.\n3. **F1 and F2 landed.** `compose_test.cljc:682-697`: both modes × {absent, `{}`}, `thrown-with-msg?` on `\"source index\"`, which is `check-common!`'s message (`compose.cljc:188-189`); P7 (the `check!` removed) fails all four. Note the fork case carries `:failure-model`/`:backend`/`:duration` it does not need; harmless, the common check runs first. F2: the three suites named in the docstring exist (`authority/grant_test`, `authority/completion_test`, `compose_rows_test`).\n4. **The world over `rows-support`.** `parked-reader-over` and `reading-writer` use `s/load-ast (s/new-machine)` (semantic-register, `rows_support.cljc:85-97`), the same AST builders as before; both `compose/open!` sites supply `:source-index (fn [] @s/source-index)` (`:793`, `:1277`), which is the composition seam the code enforces (`check-holder-config-keys!` refuses it on a holder; codex's deviation 1 is correct and the plan's \"holder-seams supplies it\" is the stale text). `offer-variant!` (`:1058-1075`) decodes the fenced pack, reverses `:yin.k/rows`, re-encodes, computes the address with `rows/pack-address` and stores under it (the jing file store verifies bytes against the address on put, which is what kills T6), then offers through h1's attributed inbound with `:yin.k/id` the variant address. Same root, same baseline, other address: exactly the owner's ruling and 7c's `variant-of`. T5 (counter-changed encoding instead) is refused `:variant-conflict`, the baseline comparison and the variant count fail: the suite now discriminates the two encodings.\n5. **Node peer build, read only.** `shadow-cljs.edn:22-24`: `:vm-bench` is a `:node-script` whose main is `bench.yin-vm-bench/-main`; the process harness overrides it with `--config-merge {:main yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-peer/main ...}` (`stage_e_process_test.clj:70-73`). No namespace allowlist or regexp exists to update. The world's new requires are `yin.vm.ucf.rows-support`, `yin.vm.ucf.rows`, `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`; rows-support pulls `yin.vm.ucf.lift-support`, `yin.vm.test-utils` (no reader conditionals at all), `yin.vm.semantic-register{,.code,.linearize}`, `dao.stream.ringbuffer`, `yin.vm.module`, `yin.vm.ledger`, `yin.vm.values`: every one a `.cljc` on the `:cljs` alias's paths (`test`, `src/cljc`). Nothing JVM-only entered the peer's closure. The compile itself is the orchestrator's.\n6. **Portability read.** No `for`, no cross-namespace private access, no new catch sites, no unary minus, no EDN strings, `(update body :yin.k/rows #(vec (reverse %)))` and `update-in` are plain. One open point, F1 below: `(sort (map first (:yin.k/rows wire)))` sorts keywords with the default comparator; the rest of the codebase sorts keyword ids by their string (`rows/pack`, `rows.cljc:719`, chosen so that \"their printed order is their byte order\").\n7. **Mutants**: table below.\n\n## Mutants (mine)\n\nDriver `target/7d-review/mut.py`; per mutant a byte backup, the actual diff, and one log per test run under `target/7d-review/mutants/<name>/`; each restore byte-compared and `git diff --quiet` on the file; the whole pass ends with `git diff --quiet -- src test` = 0. One run was interrupted mid-mutant by my own driver fix; the driver was restored from its byte backup (`git diff --quiet` = 0) before the rerun.\n\n| Mutant | File | Killed? | By |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 re-emit digest from reversed rows (Row 10 teeth) | world | yes (4) | the runner's \"re-emits the stored pack's exact canonical bytes\", row 7 failure-lower |\n| T2 `:row7/while-running` cut never matches | world | yes (3) | the runner's two cut assertions + `(:killed? l1)` of lost-authority |\n| T3 lost-authority check skipped (`destroy-authority!` a no-op) | world | yes (3) | row 7 lost-authority (refusal absent, `:final` present, ledger opens) |\n| T4 per-host fork divergence (status-policy reported `:yin.k/exclusive`) | world | yes (1) | `fork-per-host-test` |\n| T5 variant with the changed counter offered as the equal-baseline encoding | world | yes (2) | row 1: baselines differ, one variant not two |\n| T6 variant offered under the wrong pack address (the original's) | world | yes (1 error) | row 1: the store refuses the put, \"Segment address does not match the bytes\" |\n| P1 fence record omitted from the export step | driver | yes (1+1 errors) | row 7 failure-lower and row 1: \"needs the checkpoint of the granted occurrence\" |\n| P2 fence journaled before the two objects are stored (publication before durability) | driver | yes (1 error) | row 2 export phases: \"needs complete fenced snapshot\" at a pre-fence cut |\n| P3 recovery stored after the pack | driver | **survives Stage E** (row 2, 175 assertions green); killed (1) by `driver-test/the-source-half-mints-fences-and-offers-before-candidacy-test` | by design: row 2's `store-put-*`/`body-put-*` cuts are object-agnostic (plan §3.4); the put order is pinned in Stage D (7c F1) |\n| P4 report accepting an unfenced result (origin check removed) | completion | **survives Stage E** (row 7, 147 assertions green); killed (2) by `compose-rows-test/a-composed-authority-refuses-an-unfenced-result-report-test` | no Stage E life sends a wrong-origin report; the refusal is Stage C/D coverage (7c) |\n| P5 `:yin.k/root` dropped from the baseline | rows.checkpoint | yes (1 error + 3) | row 1 (\"needs valid fenced recovery\": the driver's rehydration relies on the root) and `grant-test/a-conflicting-variant-is-refused` |\n| P6 `pack` rows unsorted | rows | yes (4) | the runner's \"fenced packs have sorted rows\" |\n| P7 `open!`'s `:source-index` check removed | compose | yes (4) | `compose-test/a-source-index-is-checked-at-open-test` (7c F1 closed) |\n\nCodex's eight mutants and mine agree where they overlap (M1↔P1, M2↔T5, M3↔P2 in spirit, M4↔T3, M5↔T2, M6↔P4, M7↔T4, M8↔P7). The two survivors are outside what Stage E claims to pin and are killed by the suites that own them.\n\n## Findings\n\n| id | severity | file:line | finding | fix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | low (portability, unverified) | `test/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e_world.cljc:991` | `:sorted?` sorts row-id keywords with the default comparator, `(sort (map first …))`. Keyword comparison is portable on the JVM and Node; on ClojureDart I could not verify it from here (the distribution is outside the sandbox), and the repo sorts keyword ids by string everywhere else for exactly this reason (`rows/pack`, `rows.cljc:719`). If Dart lacks it, `stage_e_rows_test` fails on Dart only, in every scenario with a fence. | `(= (mapv (comp str first) rows) (vec (sort (map (comp str first) rows))))`, the same order `pack` uses. One line; land with 7d or as a follow-up once the orchestrator's Dart run says which. Non-blocking for the `srvm-phase7` merge; blocking for the phase boundary only if the Dart lane is red. |\n| F2 | info | `test/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e_scenarios.cljc:77-78` | The pack loop walks `:snapshots` and `:final`; the `:at-open` snapshot is stored under `:at-open` (`stage_e_world.cljc:1263`) and is skipped. Harmless: `:final` covers the same fences later in the life. | Optional: add `(get-in r [:report :at-open])` to the `concat`. |\n| F3 | info | `test/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e_world.cljc:988`, `:1075` | `:root` in the `:packs` entry and `:occurrence` in `offer-variant!`'s answer are read by no assertion (the root is compared through the baseline). Harmless observation data. | None required. |\n| F4 | info (coverage map, for 7e) | `collab/phase7-plan.md` §3.4, §3.5 | Two production orders survive Stage E alone: recovery-before-pack (P3) and the report's origin check (P4). Both are pinned by Stage D/C suites (`driver_test` 7c F1, `compose_rows_test` 7c). Not a 7d defect; worth one sentence in 7e so nobody later reads Stage E as the sole guard of either. | 7e: state which suite owns each order. |\n| F5 | info | `collab/1792500000000-engineer-7d-stage-e.report.md` \"Not run\" | Codex's note that the slow switch is unverified on Node/Dart stands; on the JVM `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` is a no-op, so every \"slow\" JVM number in codex's table and mine is the same run with or without it. | None; the orchestrator's Node/Dart runs must set the variable (they are the only hosts where it does anything). |\n\nNo production code changed in 7d and none of my mutants found a production defect. The owner's rulings hold in the tests as written: A names code and trees come only through the composition's source index (both `open!` sites, P7), the pack address is the store key and the root the semantic identity (T5, T6, P5), the only equal-baseline variant is the same root in another row order (T5), BLAKE3 throughout (`pack-address`, `digest-of`), no version dispatch or shim, lease/epoch/holder outside the body, `yin.repl` untouched, the de Bruijn kernels untouched. Codex's three deviations are all stale plan text, not code defects, and match 7c-review F3.\n\nVERDICT: READY_TO_MERGE (into the integration branch `srvm-phase7`). F1 is a one-line portability hedge decided by the orchestrator's Dart lane; everything else is informational. Tri-host acceptance of phase 7 still waits on the orchestrator's Node/Dart in-process runs and the four-pair process matrix on shiva/jupiter, as the brief assigns.\n"}}}
